statement:
You are given an integer array `arr` and an integer `d`. From index `i` you may jump to an index `j` on either side, as long as all of these hold:

- `j` lies inside the array and `1 <= |i - j| <= d`;
- `arr[j] < arr[i]` (you only jump down to a strictly lower value);
- every index `k` strictly between `i` and `j` also has `arr[k] < arr[i]` (nothing in between is as high as the starting point).

You may begin at any index and make as many jumps as you like. Return the largest number of indices you can visit in one such journey, counting the starting index.

validator:
function validate([arr, d]) {
  if (arr.length < 1 || arr.length > 1000) return 'arr needs 1 to 1000 values.';
  if (arr.some(v => !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 1 || v > 100000)) return 'Each value is a whole number from 1 to 100000.';
  if (!Number.isInteger(d) || d < 1 || d > arr.length) return 'd is a whole number from 1 to arr.length.';
  return null;
}

